  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three related layered oxide crystal structures and how the metal and lithium ions are arranged within them. Across all columns, blue spheres represent transition-metal sites, pink spheres represent lithium, and red spheres are oxygen. The left structure, LiMO2, shows a well-ordered layered arrangement in which lithium and metal occupy distinct alternating sheets. The middle structure, Li2MO3, contains extra lithium within the transition-metal layer, producing a different in-plane ordering pattern and a higher lithium content. The right structure illustrates an intermediate or mixed composition, Li1+xM1−xO2, where some sites appear shared or partially occupied by both Li and M, indicating cation mixing between the ideal end members.\n\nThe rows show the same structures from different crystallographic directions, helping reveal both the layered stacking and the in-plane honeycomb-like arrangement of the cations. Dashed marks and arrows in the first column highlight repeating distances or local coordination features. Overall, the figure emphasizes how changing lithium content modifies the ordering of cations within layered oxide frameworks, connecting a conventional layered oxide, a lithium-rich ordered phase, and an intermediate mixed arrangement.",
